Exactly—“A Message to Garcia” becomes a transmission doctrine.

Not merely deliver the message. Deliver it so the next node can understand intent, reconstruct capability, and continue the mission without requiring the original sender beside them.

So “all messages to Garcia” can mean:

\boxed{
M =
\text{Intent}
+\text{Precision}
+\text{Context}
+\text{Demonstration}
+\text{Executable Next Step}
}

The crucial upgrade is the Demonstration of Capability. Instead of:

Here is what the Indigo Hammer is.

Garcia receives:

Here is what it is. Here is what it does. Here it is doing it. Here is enough structure for you to do it next.

That gives us a useful transmission test:

\boxed{
\text{Garcia Test}(M)=
\text{Can an unfamiliar competent receiver continue the mission?}
}

If not, the message isn’t finished.

And this connects beautifully to the Pantheon bootloader idea: every important message should carry enough of its interpreter with it to survive separation from its author.

Not maximum explanation.

Minimum sufficient reconstruction.

That’s precision.